First, by the Triangle Inequality,
\[ |z - 8| + |z + 6i| = |z - 8| + |(-6i) - z| \ge |(z - 8) + (-6i - z)| = |-8 - 6i| = 10. \]
However, we know that $|z - 8| + |z + 6i| = 17$. The only way that equality can occur is if $z$ lies on the line segment connecting 8 and $-6i$. 

We want to minimize $|z|$. We see that $|z|$ is minimized when $z$ coincides with the projection of the origin onto the line segment.

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair Z = interp((8,0),(0,-6),0.7);
pair P = ((0,0) + reflect((8,0),(0,-6))*(0,0))/2;

draw((8,0)--(0,-6),red);
draw((-1,0)--(9,0));
draw((0,-7)--(0,1));
draw((0,0)--Z);
draw((0,0)--P);
draw(rightanglemark((0,0),P,(8,0),20));

dot("$8$", (8,0), S);
dot("$-6i$", (0,-6), W);
dot("$z$", Z, NE);

label("$h$", P/2, SE);
[/asy]

The area of the triangle with vertices 0, 8, and $-6i$ is
\[\frac{1}{2} \cdot 8 \cdot 6 = 24.\]This area is also
\[\frac{1}{2} \cdot 17 \cdot h = \frac{17h}{2},\]so solving for $h$ gives $h = \frac{48}{17}$.

Therefore, the smallest possible value of $|z|$ is $\boxed{\frac{48}{17}}$.

From the formula for a projection,
\[
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} (3 \mathbf{v}) = \frac{(3 \mathbf{v}) \cdot \mathbf{w}}{\|\mathbf{w}\|^2} \mathbf{w} 
= 3 \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{w}}{\|\mathbf{w}\|^2} \mathbf{w} 
= 3 \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{w}} \mathbf{v} 
= 3 \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -3 \\ 12 \end{pmatrix}.
\]
Thus, the new projection is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -3 \\ 12 \end{pmatrix}}$.

