\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 4 \\ 0 & 2 \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix} 6 & 16 \\ 0 & 2 \end{pmatrix}.\]
But we need to further simplify to get $\mathbf{B} - \mathbf{I}$, thus check,
\[\mathbf{B}^2 (\mathbf{B} - \mathbf{I}) =
\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 20 \\ 0 & 4 \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.\]
From here, similar calculations show:
\[\mathbf{B}^n (\mathbf{B} - \mathbf{I}) = \mathbf{B}^{n - 1}( \mathbf{B} - \mathbf{I}) = \dotsb = \mathbf{B} (\mathbf{B} - \mathbf{I}) = \mathbf{B} - \mathbf{I}.\]
Thus,
\[\mathbf{B}^{30}(\mathbf{B} - \mathbf{I}) = \mathbf{B} - \mathbf{I} = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}}.\]

To find a unit vector orthogonal to both $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$, we compute their cross product:
\[
\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \times \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} (1)(3) - (1)(-1) \\ (1)(1) - (3)(2) \\ (2)(-1) - (1)(1) \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 3 + 1 \\ 1 - 6 \\ -2 - 1 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -5 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}.
\]

The magnitude of this vector is $\sqrt{4^2 + (-5)^2 + (-3)^2} = \sqrt{16 + 25 + 9} = \sqrt{50} = 5\sqrt{2}$. 

To normalize this, we divide each component by $5\sqrt{2}$:
\[
\frac{1}{5\sqrt{2}} \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -5 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{4}{5\sqrt{2}} \\ \frac{-5}{5\sqrt{2}} \\ \frac{-3}{5\sqrt{2}} \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} \frac{4\sqrt{2}}{10} \\ -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\ -\frac{3\sqrt{2}}{10} \end{pmatrix}
= \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{5} \\ -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\ -\frac{3\sqrt{2}}{10} \end{pmatrix}}.
\]
